<chapter id="cloptr">
#title("Linear Closure-Functions")

<!-- ****** ****** -->

#para("

A closure-function is a boxed record that contains a pointer to an envless
function plus bindings that map certain names in the body of the envless
function to values. In practice, a function argument of a higher-order
function is often a closure-function (instead of an envless function).
Because there is no garbage collector (GC) in this dialect of ATS2, every
closure-function is #emphasis("linear"): its memory has to be freed
explicitly, and the compiler refuses to compile a program that forgets to
do so. For instance, the following higher-order function
#dyncode("list_map_cloptr") takes a linear closure-function as its second
argument:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{
a:t@ype}{b:t@ype
} list_map_cloptr{n:int}
  (xs: !list_vt (a, n), f: !(a) -<cloptr> b): list_vt (b, n)
')

The keyword #keycode("-&lt;cloptr&gt;") is used to form a type for linear
closure-functions.  Note that the symbol #keycode("!") in front of the type
of the second argument indicates that the closure-function is still
available after a call to #dyncode("list_map_cloptr") returns (it is only
borrowed).  The same symbol in front of the type of the first argument means
that the list #dyncode("xs") is borrowed, too: the result is a new list.

")#comment("para")

#para("\

The non-linear variant of closure-functions (the keyword
#keycode("-&lt;cloref&gt;"), which was used for closure-functions that are
reclaimed by GC) is not available any more: the compiler rejects every
#keycode("lam") or #keycode("fun") that would create a non-linear closure on
heap. The programmer can choose among the following alternatives:


First, a linear closure-function can be created by #keycode("lam") with
the annotation #keycode("=&lt;cloptr&gt;") (or #keycode("=&lt;cloptr1&gt;"),
or by #keycode("llam")); it is freed with #dyncode("cloptr_free"). Second, a
closure-function can be stored in the stack-frame of a function (see the
chapter on stack-allocated closure-functions), which needs no memory
allocation at all. Third, a plain envless function can be used if no
environment needs to be captured.

")#comment("para")

#para('\

A typical example making use of #dyncode("list_map_cloptr") is given as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun foo{n:int}
(
  x0: int, xs: !list_vt (int, n)
) : list_vt (int, n) = res where
{
//
val f = lam (x: int): int =<cloptr> x0 + x
val res = list_map_cloptr (xs, f)
val () = cloptr_free ($UNSAFE.castvwtp0{cloptr(void)}(f))
//
} (* end of [foo] *)
')

Note that a linear closure is first created in the body of the function
#dyncode("foo"), and it is explicitly freed after its use. The function
#dyncode("cloptr_free") is given the following interface:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun cloptr_free {a:t0p} (pclo: cloptr (a)):<!wrt> void
')

where #stacode("cloptr") is abstract. The cast
#dyncode("$UNSAFE.castvwtp0{cloptr(void)}(f)") turns the closure into a
value of the type #stacode("cloptr(void)") that
#dyncode("cloptr_free") can consume; it can certainly be replaced with
something safer but it would make programming more cumbersome. If the
call to #dyncode("cloptr_free") is dropped, the compiler complains that
the linear variable #dyncode("f") is not consumed.

')#comment("para")

#para('\

There is also some interesting interaction between currying and linear
closure-functions. In functional programming, currying means turning a
function taking multiple arguments simultaneously into a corresponding one
that takes these arguments sequentially. Let us first implement the famous
Ackermann function (that is recursive but not primitive recursive):

#dats2xhtml('\
fun
acker(m:int, n:int): int =
(
  case+ (m, n) of
  | (0, _) => n + 1
  | (m, 0) => acker (m-1, 1)
  | (_, _) => acker (m-1, acker (m, n-1))
) (* end of [acker] *)
')

A curried version of #dyncode("acker"), say, #dyncode("acker2") with the
header #dyncode("fun acker2 (m:int) (n:int): int"), would evaluate
#dyncode("acker2(3)") to a non-linear closure-function on heap, which only
GC could reclaim; so it is rejected. Instead, a curried version has to
return a linear closure-function:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun acker3 (m:int) = lam (n:int): int =<cloptr1> acker (m, n)
')

The evaluation of #dyncode("acker3(3)(4)") yields the integer 125. The
closure-function that is generated from evaluating #dyncode("acker3(3)")
is linear, and it has to be freed once it is of no more use:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = let
  val f = acker3 (3)
  val () = println! ("acker3(3)(4) = ", f(4))
in
  cloptr_free ($UNSAFE.castvwtp0{cloptr(void)}(f))
end // end of [val]
')

')#comment("para")

#para("\

Linear closure-functions play a pivotal role in supporting programming with
higher-order functions in the absence of GC. Due to advanced support for
templates in ATS2, a function argument of a higher-order function can often
be given as a template parameter instead (see the chapter on higher-order
functions), and then no closure-function is created at all. However, if
closure-functions need to be stored in a data structure, then using linear
closure-functions is the way to avoid the risk of generating memory leaks
at run-time: the data structure that contains them is linear, too, and the
type-checker makes sure that they are freed.

")#comment("para")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("chap_cloptr.dats", "on-line") the entirety of the
code used in this chapter.\

")#comment("para")

<!-- ****** ****** -->

</chapter><!--"cloptr"-->

